+// Each slot is ~14 KB and the decode thread writes every slot it fills, so a
+// ring that outgrows the cache turns those writes into misses
+// (DOL_GX_RING_SLOTS to experiment).
+// 1024 (GXRuntime recomp patch; was 64): a scene of 15,000 draws a frame
+// (Eternal Darkness's nightmare) kept a 64-slot ring full, so the decoder and
+// the main thread ran in lockstep and each one's stalls stopped the other;
+// the PC held 60 fps there only with the deeper ring (17-19 ms at 64).
+std::uint64_t kRingSlots = 1024u;
+std::unique_ptr<RenderItem[]> g_ring;
+// Each on its own cache line: the decode thread writes the tail per draw
+// while the others poll, and sharing a line would stall those writes.
